<h2 id="how-it-works">How a Udemy side hustle works</h2>
<p>Udemy is an open marketplace: anyone can apply to become an instructor, and there is no fee to publish. You create the course, set a price from Udemy's preset price tiers, and Udemy lists it in search results, recommendations and promotional emails. When someone buys, Udemy collects the money, holds it through the refund window and pays you your share.</p>
<p>There are three ways your course earns money:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Marketplace sales</strong> — a student finds and buys your course on Udemy.</li>
  <li><strong>Instructor coupon and referral sales</strong> — a student buys through a link or coupon you shared.</li>
  <li><strong>Subscription royalties</strong> — your course is included in Udemy's Personal Plan or Udemy Business, and you are paid from a royalty pool based on how many minutes subscribers watch.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Worth knowing:</strong> Coursera completed its all-stock acquisition of Udemy on May 11, 2026, according to Coursera's SEC filings. The two platforms were still running separately at closing, and Udemy told instructors that existing terms were not changing for now. Keep an eye on instructor announcements over the next year.</div>

<h2 id="instructor-payment">Udemy instructor payment and revenue share (2026)</h2>
<p>This is the part that decides whether a Udemy side hustle is worth your time. Udemy's published revenue share, as of 2026, breaks down like this:</p>
