<p>The subscription share is the one that has moved. In a November 2023 notice, Udemy scheduled the instructor pool to drop from 25% to 20% in 2024, 17.5% in 2025 and 15% in January 2026, while leaving the 37% marketplace share unchanged. If most of your income comes from subscribers, expect it to be smaller per minute than it was a few years ago.</p>
<p>The practical lesson: <strong>the 97% coupon rate is where the money is</strong>. Every student you send yourself, from a YouTube channel, newsletter, LinkedIn post or blog, is worth roughly 2.6 times more than a student Udemy finds for you.</p>

<h3 id="payouts">How and when Udemy pays you</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Methods:</strong> PayPal or Payoneer, with direct deposit available for US instructors. Payout vendors can charge their own transaction fees.</li>
  <li><strong>Schedule:</strong> Monthly, usually within the first 10 business days of the month.</li>
  <li><strong>Hold period:</strong> Students have 30 days to request a refund, so earnings are held about 30 days after the month they were made. A January sale is typically paid in early March.</li>
  <li><strong>Threshold:</strong> Since September 2025, Udemy only sends a payout once your balance reaches $25. Balances below that roll over.</li>
  <li><strong>Taxes:</strong> You must submit a tax form (W-9 for US instructors) before you are paid. Udemy income is self-employment income, so set money aside; the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> shows how much.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="requirements">Udemy course requirements and pricing</h2>
<p>Udemy runs every new course through a quality review before it goes live. For a <strong>paid</strong> course, you need:</p>
<ul>
  <li>At least <strong>30 minutes of video</strong> in total.</li>
  <li>At least <strong>five lectures</strong>.</li>
  <li>HD video (720p or higher) with clear audio that is synced to the picture.</li>
  <li>A complete landing page: title, subtitle, description, learning objectives, an image and a promo video is strongly recommended.</li>
  <li>An approved premium instructor account with payout and tax details.</li>
</ul>
<p>Free courses are capped at two hours of video and do not pay. They can still be useful to collect reviews and build an audience you can later market a paid course to.</p>
<h3 id="pricing">How pricing works</h3>
<p>You choose a list price from Udemy's preset price tiers. In reality, most marketplace sales happen during Udemy's frequent site-wide promotions, when courses drop to low sale prices. Some instructors opt into Udemy's deals program to be included; others stay out to protect their price. Your instructor coupons let you set your own discounts on top.</p>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Do not price based on the list price you see on other courses. Look at what similar courses actually sell for during promotions, because that is closer to what most of your buyers will pay.</div>

<h2 id="income">How much can you make on Udemy?</h2>
<p>Udemy does not publish average instructor income, and the figures you see on YouTube come from the outliers. Here is the honest version: a first course in a crowded niche with no outside audience often earns somewhere from nothing to a few dozen dollars a month. A well-made course on an in-demand skill with good reviews can earn a few hundred dollars a month for years. The instructors earning full-time income typically have many courses, a clear niche, and their own email list or channel feeding coupon sales.</p>
