<h2 id="get-started">How to start a Udemy side hustle: step by step</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick a teachable skill with proven demand.</strong> Search Udemy for your topic and look at the bestsellers: number of students, number of reviews and when they were last updated. Lots of reviews means real demand. Outdated top courses mean an opening.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrow it down.</strong> "Excel" is saturated. "Excel for property managers" or "Power Query for accountants" is easier to win. Specific outcomes sell better than broad overviews.</li>
  <li><strong>Outline before you record.</strong> Write the end result a student gets, then break it into sections and 3–10 minute lectures. Each lecture should teach one thing.</li>
  <li><strong>Set up a simple recording space.</strong> Clean audio matters more than video. A decent USB mic, soft light and a quiet room beat an expensive camera.</li>
  <li><strong>Record and edit in batches.</strong> Screen recordings work for software and business topics. Cut long pauses and mistakes, but do not chase perfection.</li>
  <li><strong>Build a landing page that sells.</strong> Use the words students search for in your title, list concrete outcomes and record a 1–2 minute promo video.</li>
  <li><strong>Submit for review and publish.</strong> Fix anything flagged in the quality review, then launch.</li>
  <li><strong>Drive your first students with coupons.</strong> Share an instructor coupon with your network, a relevant community or your audience to get early enrollments and honest reviews.</li>
  <li><strong>Improve and expand.</strong> Answer Q&amp;A, update outdated lectures and build a second course in the same niche so students can buy from you again.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="recording-setup">Recording setup for Udemy courses</h2>
<p>You do not need a studio. Most successful courses are recorded at a desk with a USB microphone, a webcam for talking-head intros and a light in front of you. A [[amazon:usb-mic|USB condenser microphone]] is the single biggest upgrade, because Udemy reviewers and students will forgive average video but not echoey or noisy sound.</p>
<p>[[gear:usb-mic,ring-light,webcam|Recording setup for course creators]]</p>
<p>Record in a small room with soft furnishings, turn off fans and notifications, and do a 30-second test before every session.</p>

<h2 id="promotion">How to promote your Udemy course</h2>
<p>Udemy's algorithm favors courses that already sell and get good reviews, so your first few weeks matter. A few tactics that work:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Use instructor coupons on your own channels.</strong> They earn you 97% and help the course gain momentum.</li>
